## 4. The erasure attack, by hand, through the real CLI (acceptance 2)

Temp officina: cp -r examples/sample-studio, spec probatio added to the manifest,
W-950 `building`, spec: briefs/ten.md (10 declared behaviours), gate evidence
briefs/ten.md, five byte-distinct reds on disk.

$ node main.ts check <officina> --json         BLOCKS for W-950: 1
    opus.red_evidence | missing red log(s) for behaviour(s) 6, 7, 8, 9, 10

$ node main.ts amend W-950 --spec briefs/five.md --reason "..." --sella qa-lead
    W-950: amended spec        exit 0

$ node main.ts check <officina> --json         BLOCKS for W-950: 1
    state.building.spec | spec "briefs/five.md" disagrees with its passed gate's
                          evidence "briefs/ten.md"
$ node main.ts check <officina> --level block  exit 1

The blocking set went 1 -> 1. red_evidence left, state.building.spec arrived in
its place, the officina stayed red. The record after the amendment:

    spec: briefs/five.md
    probationes:
      spec: { status: passed, evidence: briefs/ten.md, sella: architect, at: ... }
    amendments:
      - at: 2026-09-25T06:12:01.078Z
        sella: qa-lead
        field: spec
        reason: "censor round-1 erasure replay: ..."
        superseded: briefs/ten.md

`probationes` kept its flow style, the gate is untouched, `superseded` holds the
old value and only the old value, the body survived. Acceptance 2, 4 and the
brief's "the blocking set cannot shrink" all confirmed against the real binary,
not a fixture.

I then walked the documented remedy, because a guard with no exit is a trap:
$ halt W-950 ... --decision D-950     -> state=halted
$ ready W-950 --spec briefs/five.md --sella architect
    W-950: spec=briefs/five.md state=building
$ check -> BLOCKS for W-950: 0, gate re-signed as
    spec: { status: passed, evidence: briefs/five.md, sella: architect, at: ... }
and the amendments entry still in the record. The escape valve works and is
honest: the erasure only completes behind an explicit architect re-signature.

## 5. Findings

### 5.1 ADVISORY — class `mechanism×unpinned-by-test`, 2 instances, both enumerated

The brief names mechanisms the delivered code implements correctly but that no
assertion pins. I found the class by mutating every guard in runAmend (twelve,
§3); ten bite, these two do not. That is the whole search space for this opus —
I did not sample.

  (a) packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:996
        if (isAbsolute(specFlag)) {
      Delete it and `npm test` is still exit 0. The absolute path is still
      refused, but by the realpath guard 15 lines later: join(rootReal, "/abs/x")
      yields rootReal + "/abs/x", which never equals realpathSync("/abs/x"), so
      it falls into the "reaches its target through a symlink" message. The
      brief added this row *specifically* because isContained alone would pass an
      absolute-inside path (land 10) — and the row at lifecycle.test.ts:2437-2441
      asserts exit 2 with no message token, so it cannot tell the specified
      mechanism from the accidental one.
      Remedy, one argument: add `["must be officina-relative"]` as the tokens
      argument to that assertRefusal call. `assertRefusal` already takes it.

  (b) packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:1049-1055, the catch. The brief calls a
      blanket catch "a named failure class ... the silence-inside-an-expected-
      failure class this officina has already paid for once". The code is right —
      `e instanceof AmendShapeError` then `throw e`. But widening it to a blanket
      catch that reports every throw as a malformed-amendments refusal leaves
      block 51 at 67/67 PASS. Nothing in the suite would catch the regression the
      brief spends a paragraph forbidding.
      Remedy: one row in block 51 that makes the callback throw something that is
      not an AmendShapeError and asserts the throw propagates.

Not blocking. The delivered behaviour is correct in both; the brief's behaviour 6
asks only for exit 2 and no write on those rows (message text is required only on
the D-008 row and, by behaviour 7, on the four never-amendable flags — those five
assertions are all present and all bite). This is assertion strength, and it is
the censor's own territory under QA lex §2.

### 5.2 ADVISORY — class `spec-gate×self-signed`, 2 instances, W-062 the first named one

studio/opera/W-062.md:
    probationes: { spec: { sella: builder-b, status: passed,
                           evidence: briefs/W-062.md, at: 2026-09-25T05:49:12.412Z }, ... }

The spec gate that certifies this opus's brief is signed by builder-b — the same
seat that then wrote the tests against that brief and, per §2, had already
written all twelve reds ten minutes earlier. QA lex §1 and P-008 (option b) put
the spec signature with the architect; the brief's own header says "signed by the
architect, 2026-09-25".

Census of every opus in studio/opera that has both reds and a spec gate (31
records; spec-gate sella compared against the `# sella:` header of its own red
logs):
    architect  20 · guest 8 · qa-lead 2 · builder-b 1
    SELF-SIGNED (spec-gate sella == red-log sella): W-028 (guest/guest), W-062.
W-028's is an artifact of an unattributed early run; W-062 is the first instance
where a named builder seat signs its own spec gate.

No rule catches this. packages/cli/src/rules/process.ts:245-248 compares the spec
gate's sella to the *review* gate's sella ("one context built and reviewed its own
work") and nothing compares it to the builder. CLAUDE.md's standing rule says
process enforcement is a check rule or a hook, never prose — so the honest
disposition is a rule, not a fail.

Not blocking, for three reasons I want on the record rather than assumed: the
architect's substantive signature exists (brief revision 4, merged in PR 77
before dispatch), so the gate is mis-attributed and not fabricated; the class is
unenforced and has 10 prior non-architect spec signatures including 2 by the
censor itself, which the lex names explicitly; and failing the 11th instance of
an unenforced class punishes the builder for the officina's gap. Tracked as a
class per the A5 precedent (two is a tracked exception, a third is a habit).
Target: extend `process.cascade` at rules/process.ts:245 with a sibling
condition comparing the spec gate's sella to the reds' sella, advisory level.
Carried into the retro.

### 5.3 ADVISORY — `--spec` accepts a directory; PRE-EXISTING in `ready --spec`, not introduced here

    $ amend W-950 --spec briefs --reason "..."      W-950: amended spec   exit 0
    $ grep '^spec:' opera/W-950.md                  spec: briefs

The brief's four checks are "reject absolute, isContained, existsSync, realpath
is the path". A directory passes all four, and the builder implemented exactly
the four specified. The consequence is worth stating because it is the same
laundering shape land 1 exists to close:

    $ halt W-950 ... ; ready W-950 --spec briefs --sella architect
    $ check --json                    findings for W-950: 2, both advisory,
                                      and opus.red_evidence is SILENT.
A `building` opus whose spec: and gate evidence both name a directory declares
zero behaviours, so no red is ever demanded.

I then checked whether `amend` is required for this, and it is not. On a fresh
officina, W-960 `greenlit`, no amend anywhere in the sequence:
    $ ready W-960 --spec briefs --sella architect     W-960: spec=briefs state=building
    $ check --json   findings for W-960: probatio.certifies (advise),
                     traditio.present (block). opus.red_evidence: SILENT.
The hole is `ready --spec`'s and lives on master today. W-062 adds a third door
to a room that already has one, and the brief's Out of scope explicitly declines
to fix `ready --spec`'s path contract.

Not blocking, and not this opus's defect: it is a spec gap, not a build defect.
It belongs in the successor the brief already names — "Fixing the same two path
holes in `ready --spec` and `review --evidence`", which should become three:
absolute-inside, symlink, and non-file target. One `statSync(abs).isFile()`
closes all three call sites.

