### 5.5 NIT — block 51 folds two brief rows into one

The brief lists both "`--spec` equal to the current pointer" and the rev-2 alias
row "`--spec ./briefs/<id>.md` where spec: is already briefs/<id>.md". Block 51
has one row (lifecycle.test.ts:2436), the alias variant. It is the strictly
stronger of the two — M8 proves it fails the moment the comparison stops being
resolve()-based — so nothing is lost. Recorded so the row count is not read as
a missing row.

### 5.6 NIT — the equality condition's message overstates

packages/cli/src/check.ts:563-564 fires on `specPath && specEvidence`, without
reading the gate's status, but the message says "its passed gate's evidence".
On a `building` opus with a failed-or-pending spec gate that still carries an
`evidence` key, both clauses of state.building.spec fire and the second one
calls the gate passed when it is not. The brief specifies the condition exactly
as implemented ("when both spec: and probationes.spec.evidence are present"), so
the code is right and the wording is loose. Cosmetic.

## 6. Rulings on the builder's four disclosures

1. Reds before `ready`. NOT a red-provenance issue — see §2. The reds are honest
   by content, by tree hash, by distinctness and by the mutation evidence in §3.
   No rule orders the two. Harmless sequencing slip. What travels with it — that
   the builder then signed the spec gate itself — is §5.2 and a different class.

2. Behaviour 10 in usage.test.ts's whole-file guard rather than a duplicate
   block. CORRECT, and the brief says so in as many words at :566-568: "both
   asserted by that file's existing guard, which fails if either half is
   missed". The red is recorded against that file (10.log, `# command: node
   --import tsx packages/commands/src/usage.test.ts .`), FAILing on both halves —
   the AMEND_USAGE banner line and `seen === 37`. A duplicate block in
   lifecycle.test.ts would have been a second assertion of one fact and a second
   place to forget. Nothing to fix.

3. containment.test.ts touched though un-named. CORRECT AND MINIMAL, and it does
   not exceed the writ. W-047's census is a tripwire whose entire purpose is to
   fire when a new `safeItemPath` call site appears; deleting the added PINNED_A
   line reproduces the failure exactly — "pinned=14 scan=15 ... added:
   [packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:runAmend:opera#1]". Updating the pinned
   inventory IS the response the tripwire asks for; the alternative (a bypass) is
   what W-047 says needs a decision. The builder also added the traversal row
   (lifecycle.test.ts:2169-2190, row 10 of block 45), which is the half that
   actually proves the new call site refuses `../W-777` and touches neither
   sentinel. Silencing would have been the violation; this is the opposite.

4. The erasure attack replayed by hand. CONFIRMED INDEPENDENTLY — §4, run through
   the real CLI on my own temp officina, 1 block before and 1 block after, and
   M3 in §3 shows the anti-shrink assertion goes to "11 -> 10" and fails the
   moment the equality condition is removed. The builder's 8-and-8 figure differs
   from my 1-and-1 only because its fixture's five reds were near-identical and
   drew ten `opus.red_content` blocks as padding; mine were byte-distinct. The
   property is the same and holds either way.

## 7. Verdict

PASS. Every one of the 12 behaviours is present and asserted; the six mechanisms
the brief singles out — isSeq on the Document node inside the callback,
AmendShapeError caught alone, realpath-is-the-path anchored on the officina's own
realpath, the equality condition folded into state.building.spec with rules/ids.ts
untouched, append-only amendments with superseded holding only the old value, and
W-040's corrections shape — are all implemented as written, and all but the
blanket-catch prohibition are pinned by a mutation I ran. Acceptances 1 through 7
hold, the last two by replay through the real binary. No blocking finding.

The three advisories are for the follow-on, not for this opus: two assertion-
strength gaps the censor can close in a test-only change (§5.1), a spec-gate
attribution class for the retro (§5.2), and a path hole that predates this opus
in a file it was told not to touch (§5.3).
